/-- Without coupling the covariance need not converge. There are reflection-symmetric cell data
$(\gamma_x)$ on the chain with one mode per site satisfying the Fourier state condition,
$\hat\gamma(k) + i\Omega \ge 0$ for every $k$, such that for the uncoupled example rule
$L_{\pi/2, 0}$ (a quarter turn at every site, $\lambda = 0$) the position-position entry of the
evolved cell at the origin, $(\gamma^{(t)}_0)_{qq}$ with $\gamma^{(t)} = (L_{\pi/2, 0*})^t\gamma$,
does not converge as $t \to \infty$. -/
theorem dyn_no_coupling :
    ∃ γc : ℤ → Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ, CellData γc ∧
      (∀ x, γc (-x) = γc x) ∧
      (∀ k, (gammaHat γc k +
        Complex.I • (Gaussian.symplecticForm 1).map Complex.ofReal).PosSemidef) ∧
      ¬ ∃ c : ℝ, Tendsto (fun t : ℕ =>
        (evolveCells (rotationSymbol (Real.pi / 2) 0))^[t] γc 0 (Sum.inl 0) (Sum.inl 0)) atTop
          (𝓝 c) := sorry

open QCALean.Bosonic in
open Filter Topology in
/-- Convergence of the states (the weak-* part of thesis Theorem 4.9). Let $(\varphi, \lambda)$
lie in the regime, let $(\gamma_x)$ be cell data on the chain with one mode per site, and let
$\omega$ be a Gaussian state of the CCR algebra of the lattice phase space with covariance form the
form of $(\gamma_x)$ and displacement $0$. For $t \in \mathbb N$ let $\alpha_t$ be the star
automorphism of the CCR algebra with $\alpha_t(W(u)) = W(L_{\varphi, \lambda}^t u)$ (the
automorphism of the symplectic rule $L^t_{\varphi, \lambda}$), and $\omega_t = \omega \circ
\alpha_t$ the evolved Gaussian state. Then the characteristic functions converge pointwise,
$\omega_t(W(u)) \to \exp(-\gamma_\infty(u, u)/4)$ for every $u$, with $\gamma_\infty$ the form of
the limit cells, and there is a Gaussian state $\omega_\infty$ with covariance form
$\gamma_\infty$ and displacement $0$ such that $\omega_t(a) \to \omega_\infty(a)$ for every element
$a$ of the CCR algebra. (The symplecticity of the powers, supplied as a hypothesis, always
holds.) -/
theorem dyn_limit_state {φ lam : ℝ} (hr : ExampleRegime φ lam)
    (hL : ∀ t : ℕ, IsSymplectic (rotationSymbol φ lam ^ t))
    {γc : ℤ → Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ} (h : CellData γc)
    (ω : GaussianState (sigma ℤ 1)) (hcov : ω.cov = cellForm γc) (hdisp : ω.disp = 0) :
    (∀ (t : ℕ) (a : CCR (sigma ℤ 1)),
      (ω.evolve (hL t)).toCLM a = ω.toCLM (ruleAlpha (hL t) a)) ∧
    (∀ (t : ℕ) (u : Core.LatticeSpace ℝ ℤ (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1)),
      ruleAlpha (hL t) (wCCR (sigma ℤ 1) u) =
        wCCR (sigma ℤ 1) (Matrix.toLin' (rotationSymbol φ lam ^ t) u)) ∧
    (∀ u : Core.LatticeSpace ℝ ℤ (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1),
      Tendsto (fun t : ℕ => (ω.evolve (hL t)).charFun u) atTop
        (𝓝 (Complex.exp (-(cellForm (limitCells φ lam γc) u u : ℂ) / 4)))) ∧
    ∃ ωinf : GaussianState (sigma ℤ 1), ωinf.cov = cellForm (limitCells φ lam γc) ∧
      ωinf.disp = 0 ∧ ∀ a : CCR (sigma ℤ 1),
        Tendsto (fun t : ℕ => (ω.evolve (hL t)).toCLM a) atTop (𝓝 (ωinf.toCLM a)) := sorry

/-! ## Gaussian channels and irreversible automata on the chain

For real bilinear forms $\beta$, $\beta'$ on a real vector space $V$, the condition
$\beta + i\beta' \ge 0$ means that for every finite family $u_1, \dots, u_m$ in $V$ the complex
matrix $(\beta(u_j, u_k) + i\beta'(u_j, u_k))_{jk}$ is positive semidefinite. A completely
positive map of a C*-algebra is a linear map all of whose matrix amplifications are positive. On
the chain, $\tau_a$ is the translation of the lattice phase space by $a \in \mathbb Z$, the
lattice CCR net assigns to $O \subseteq \mathbb Z$ the local algebra $\mathcal A(O)$, and the
translations act on the CCR algebra by the star automorphisms $\alpha_a$ with
$\alpha_a(W(u)) = W(\tau_a u)$. The range $N_M$ of a symbol $M$ is the set of $m$ at which some
entry has a nonzero coefficient. Statements about lattice channels concern the channels with the
given data; the existence of a Gaussian channel with prescribed data is not asserted. -/

open scoped CStarAlgebra ComplexOrder in
/-- Gaussian channels (thesis §4.3). Let $\sigma$ be a real bilinear form on a real vector space
$V$. A Gaussian channel on $\mathrm{CCR}(V, \sigma)$ consists of a linear map $L : V \to V$, a
bilinear form $\nu$ (the noise), a linear form $\mu$ (the displacement) and a completely positive
map $f$ of the CCR algebra; such a quadruple is a Gaussian channel exactly when $\nu$ is
symmetric, the channel condition (4.34) $\nu + i(\sigma - \sigma(L\cdot, L\cdot)) \ge 0$ holds,
and $f(W(u)) = \exp(i\mu(u) - \nu(u, u)/4)\, W(Lu)$ for every $u \in V$. For bilinear forms
$\beta$, $\beta'$ on $V$, the condition $\beta + i\beta' \ge 0$ is the condition on Gram matrices
stated at the head of this section. The concatenation
$T_2 \circ T_1$ of two Gaussian channels has the map $a \mapsto f_2(f_1(a))$, the linear map
$L_2 L_1$, the noise $\nu_1 + \nu_2(L_1\cdot, L_1\cdot)$ and the displacement
$\mu_1 + \mu_2 \circ L_1$. -/
theorem dyn_gaussian_channel {V : Type*} [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V]
    (σ : LinearMap.BilinForm ℝ V) :
    (∀ (L : V →ₗ[ℝ] V) (ν : LinearMap.BilinForm ℝ V) (μ : V →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ) (f : CCR σ →CP CCR σ),
      (∃ T : GaussianChannel σ, T.lin = L ∧ T.noise = ν ∧ T.disp = μ ∧ T.map = f) ↔
        ν.IsSymm ∧ IsStateConditionC ν (σ - σ.comp L L) ∧
        ∀ u, f (wCCR σ u) =
          Complex.exp (Complex.I * (μ u : ℂ) - (ν u u : ℂ) / 4) • wCCR σ (L u)) ∧
    (∀ β β' : LinearMap.BilinForm ℝ V, IsStateConditionC β β' ↔ ∀ (m : ℕ) (u : Fin m → V),
      (Matrix.of fun j k => (β (u j) (u k) : ℂ) + Complex.I * (β' (u j) (u k) : ℂ)).PosSemidef) ∧
    ∀ T₁ T₂ : GaussianChannel σ, (T₂.comp T₁).lin = T₂.lin ∘ₗ T₁.lin ∧
      (T₂.comp T₁).noise = T₁.noise + T₂.noise.comp T₁.lin T₁.lin ∧
      (T₂.comp T₁).disp = T₁.disp + T₂.disp ∘ₗ T₁.lin ∧
      ∀ a, (T₂.comp T₁).map a = T₂.map (T₁.map a) := sorry

open QCALean.Bosonic in
open scoped CStarAlgebra in
/-- Lattice channels (thesis p. 92) are quantum cellular automata. Let $T$ be a Gaussian channel
on the CCR algebra of the lattice phase space of the chain with $n$ modes per site, $M$ a symbol
and $(\nu_x)$ a family of real $2n \times 2n$ matrices. $T$ is a lattice channel with rule $M$ and
noise cells $(\nu_x)$ exactly when its linear map is $u \mapsto Mu$, its noise is the form of
$(\nu_x)$, the family $(\nu_x)$ is cell data, and its displacement is translation invariant,
$\mu(\tau_a u) = \mu(u)$ for all $a$ and $u$. A lattice channel commutes with the translations,
$\alpha_a \circ T = T \circ \alpha_a$, and there is a quantum cellular automaton on the lattice
CCR net, covariant under the translations, with rule $T$ and neighbourhood the range $N_M$. -/
theorem dyn_lattice_channel {n : ℕ} (T : GaussianChannel (phaseSpace ℤ n).sigma)
    (M : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (AddMonoidAlgebra ℝ ℤ))
    (g : ℤ → Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) ℝ) :
    (IsLatticeChannel T M g ↔ T.lin = (Matrix.toLin' M).restrictScalars ℝ ∧
      T.noise = cellForm g ∧ CellData g ∧
      ∀ (a : ℤ) (u : Core.LatticeSpace ℝ ℤ (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n)),
        T.disp (Core.translate ℝ ℤ _ a u) = T.disp u) ∧
    (IsLatticeChannel T M g →
      (∀ (a : Multiplicative ℤ) (x : CCR (phaseSpace ℤ n).sigma),
        ccrTranslate ℤ n a (T.map x) = T.map (ccrTranslate ℤ n a x)) ∧
      ∃ Q : Net.QCA (LinearAQFT.Symplectic.ccrNet (phaseSpace ℤ n)) (ccrTranslate ℤ n),
        Q.rule = T.map ∧ Q.nbhd = Core.symbolRange M) := sorry

open QCALean.Bosonic in
/-- Locality of lattice channels (thesis Lemma 4.11, in both directions). Let $M$ be a symbol on the
chain with $n$ modes per site. The pull-back cells of the on-site cells $\delta_{\cdot,0}\Omega$
along $M$ are the coefficient matrices of $M^{\star T}\Omega M$, and their form is
$\sigma(M\cdot, M\cdot)$. Let $T$ be a lattice channel with rule $M$ and noise cells $(\nu_x)$.
(I) For every set $N \subseteq \mathbb Z$, $T$ has finite propagation with neighbourhood $N$
exactly when $N_M \subseteq N$. (II) For every finite set $M' \subseteq \mathbb Z$ with
$0 \in M'$ and $-x \in M'$ for every $x \in M'$, $T$ factorizes with respect to $M'$ exactly when
$\nu_x = 0$ for every $x \notin M'$ and the range of $M^{\star T}\Omega M$ is contained in $M'$
(the cells of $\sigma(M\cdot, M\cdot)$ vanish outside $M'$; thesis (4.36)). -/
theorem dyn_locality {n : ℕ}
    (M : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (AddMonoidAlgebra ℝ ℤ)) :
    (∀ d : ℤ, pullbackKernel M (onSiteKernel (Gaussian.symplecticForm n)) d =
      coeffMat d (Core.starTranspose M * Core.const ℤ (Gaussian.symplecticForm n) * M)) ∧
    cellForm (pullbackKernel M (onSiteKernel (Gaussian.symplecticForm n))) =
      ((phaseSpace ℤ n).sigma).comp ((Matrix.toLin' M).restrictScalars ℝ)
        ((Matrix.toLin' M).restrictScalars ℝ) ∧
    ∀ (T : GaussianChannel (phaseSpace ℤ n).sigma)
      (g : ℤ → Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) ℝ),
      IsLatticeChannel T M g →
        (∀ N : Set ℤ, Net.IsLocalWith (LinearAQFT.Symplectic.ccrNet (phaseSpace ℤ n)) T.map N ↔
          (Core.symbolRange M : Set ℤ) ⊆ N) ∧
        ∀ M' : Finset ℤ, 0 ∈ M' → (∀ x ∈ M', -x ∈ M') →
          (Net.IsFactorizing (LinearAQFT.Symplectic.ccrNet (phaseSpace ℤ n)) T.map (M' : Set ℤ) ↔
            (∀ x ∉ M', g x = 0) ∧ Core.symbolRange
              (Core.starTranspose M * Core.const ℤ (Gaussian.symplecticForm n) * M) ⊆ M') := sorry

open QCALean.Bosonic in
/-- Factorization does not imply finite propagation, and the converse separation for the rule $0$
holds for every lattice channel with the given data (thesis Lemma 4.10(v)); the existence of such
a channel is not proved. Let $n \ge 1$ be the number of modes per site. (a) There is a Gaussian
channel that is a lattice channel with the shift as rule (the symbol $z \cdot 1$) and noise cells
$0$, that factorizes with respect to $\{0\}$, and that does not have finite propagation with
neighbourhood $\{0\}$. (b) For every finite set
$M' \subseteq \mathbb Z$ there are cell data $(\nu_x)$ of finite range, $\nu_m = 0$ for
$|m| > R$ for some $R \in \mathbb N$, such that the rule $0$ and the noise $\nu$ satisfy the channel
condition $\nu + i(\sigma - \sigma(0\cdot, 0\cdot)) \ge 0$, and no lattice channel with rule $0$ and
noise cells $(\nu_x)$ factorizes with respect to $M'$. (That such a channel exists rests on the
characterization of Gaussian channels by their data, thesis Thm. 2.6, which is not asserted
here.) -/
theorem dyn_independence (n : ℕ) [NeZero n] :
    (∃ T : GaussianChannel (phaseSpace ℤ n).sigma,
      IsLatticeChannel T (Matrix.diagonal fun _ => AddMonoidAlgebra.single 1 1) 0 ∧
      Net.IsFactorizing (LinearAQFT.Symplectic.ccrNet (phaseSpace ℤ n)) T.map {0} ∧
      ¬ Net.IsLocalWith (LinearAQFT.Symplectic.ccrNet (phaseSpace ℤ n)) T.map {0}) ∧
    ∀ M' : Finset ℤ, ∃ g : ℤ → Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) ℝ,
      CellData g ∧ (∃ R : ℕ, ∀ m, (R : ℤ) < |m| → g m = 0) ∧
      IsStateConditionC (cellForm g)
        ((phaseSpace ℤ n).sigma - ((phaseSpace ℤ n).sigma).comp 0 0) ∧
      ∀ T : GaussianChannel (phaseSpace ℤ n).sigma, IsLatticeChannel T 0 g →
        ¬ Net.IsFactorizing (LinearAQFT.Symplectic.ccrNet (phaseSpace ℤ n)) T.map (M' : Set ℤ) :=
  sorry

open QCALean.Bosonic in
open scoped Pointwise Matrix in
/-- Concatenation of lattice channels (thesis p. 96). If $T_1$ and $T_2$ are lattice channels on the
chain with $n$ modes per site, with rules $L_1$, $L_2$ and noise cells $\nu_1$, $\nu_2$, then
$T_2 \circ T_1$ is a lattice channel with rule $L_2 L_1$ and noise cells $\nu_1 + L_{1*}\nu_2$.
For all symbols $L$, $L_1$, $L_2$ and families $\nu$, $\nu_1$, $\nu_2$ of real $2n \times 2n$
matrices: the form of $L_*\nu$ is $\nu(L\cdot, L\cdot)$, with $\nu$ also denoting the form of the
family; $N_{L_2 L_1} \subseteq N_{L_1} + N_{L_2}$; if $\nu_x = 0$ outside a finite set $A$, then
$(L_*\nu)_d = 0$ for $d \notin A + (N_L - N_L)$; the range of $L^{\star T}\Omega L$ is contained in
$N_L - N_L$; if $N_1$, $N_2$ are finite sets, $N_2$ nonempty, $N_{L_i} \subseteq N_i$ and
$\nu_{i, x} = 0$ for $x \notin N_i - N_i$ ($i = 1, 2$), then $N_{L_2 L_1} \subseteq N_1 + N_2$ and
$\nu_1 + L_{1*}\nu_2$ vanishes outside $(N_1 + N_2) - (N_1 + N_2)$; and for on-site cells
$\delta_{\cdot, 0} a$, $\delta_{\cdot, 0} b$ of real matrices $a$, $b$, the cells
$\delta_{\cdot, 0} a + L_{1*}(\delta_{\cdot, 0} b)$ vanish off the origin exactly when
$L_{1*}(\delta_{\cdot, 0} b)$ does (4.39). The thesis writes the
ranges of the composite as equalities; the statement gives the inclusions. -/
theorem dyn_concatenation {n : ℕ} :
    (∀ {T₁ T₂ : GaussianChannel (phaseSpace ℤ n).sigma}
      {L₁ L₂ : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (AddMonoidAlgebra ℝ ℤ)}
      {g₁ g₂ : ℤ → Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) ℝ},
      IsLatticeChannel T₁ L₁ g₁ → IsLatticeChannel T₂ L₂ g₂ →
        IsLatticeChannel (T₂.comp T₁) (L₂ * L₁) (g₁ + pullbackKernel L₁ g₂)) ∧
    (∀ (L : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (AddMonoidAlgebra ℝ ℤ))
      (g : ℤ → Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) ℝ),
      cellForm (pullbackKernel L g) =
        (cellForm g).comp ((Matrix.toLin' L).restrictScalars ℝ)
          ((Matrix.toLin' L).restrictScalars ℝ)) ∧
    (∀ L₁ L₂ : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (AddMonoidAlgebra ℝ ℤ),
      Core.symbolRange (L₂ * L₁) ⊆ Core.symbolRange L₁ + Core.symbolRange L₂) ∧
    (∀ (L : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (AddMonoidAlgebra ℝ ℤ))
      (g : ℤ → Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) ℝ) (A : Finset ℤ),
      (∀ x ∉ A, g x = 0) → ∀ d ∉ A + (Core.symbolRange L - Core.symbolRange L),
        pullbackKernel L g d = 0) ∧
    (∀ L : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (AddMonoidAlgebra ℝ ℤ),
      Core.symbolRange (Core.starTranspose L * Core.const ℤ (Gaussian.symplecticForm n) * L) ⊆
        Core.symbolRange L - Core.symbolRange L) ∧
    (∀ {L₁ L₂ : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (AddMonoidAlgebra ℝ ℤ)}
      {g₁ g₂ : ℤ → Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) ℝ} (N₁ N₂ : Finset ℤ),
      Core.symbolRange L₁ ⊆ N₁ → (∀ x ∉ N₁ - N₁, g₁ x = 0) → Core.symbolRange L₂ ⊆ N₂ →
      (∀ x ∉ N₂ - N₂, g₂ x = 0) → N₂.Nonempty →
        Core.symbolRange (L₂ * L₁) ⊆ N₁ + N₂ ∧
          ∀ x ∉ (N₁ + N₂) - (N₁ + N₂), (g₁ + pullbackKernel L₁ g₂) x = 0) ∧
    ∀ (L₁ : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (AddMonoidAlgebra ℝ ℤ))
      (a b : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) ℝ),
      (∀ x ≠ 0, (onSiteKernel a + pullbackKernel L₁ (onSiteKernel b)) x = 0) ↔
        ∀ x ≠ 0, pullbackKernel L₁ (onSiteKernel b) x = 0 := sorry

open QCALean.Bosonic in
open scoped Matrix in
/-- The example system with noise: two steps with on-site noise (thesis p. 97, corrected) and a
lossy chain. Let $r = (\sin\varphi, \cos\varphi)^T$, and for a real $2 \times 2$ matrix $\nu_0$ let
$\delta_{\cdot, 0}\nu_0$ be the on-site cells. (a) The pull-back cell at distance two is
$(L_{\varphi, \lambda *}\delta_{\cdot, 0}\nu_0)_2 = (\nu_0)_{qq}\, \lambda^2\, r r^T$. (b) If
$\nu_0$ is positive semidefinite, $(L_{\varphi, \lambda *}\delta_{\cdot, 0}\nu_0)_x = 0$ for every
$x \neq 0$ exactly when $\lambda = 0$ or $(\nu_0)_{qq} = 0$. By (4.39) these pull-back cells
decide whether the noise of two steps with on-site noise $\nu_0$ stays on site, and the claim of the
thesis that for nonzero coupling it does not holds for isotropic noise and fails for
$\operatorname{diag}(0, b)$: (c) for $\lambda \neq 0$ and $c > 0$ the cell at distance two for
$\nu_0 = c\, 1$ is nonzero; (d) for $\nu_0 = \operatorname{diag}(0, b)$ with $b \ge 0$ the pull-back
cells vanish off the origin, for every $\lambda$. (e) The lossy chain: for $0 < \eta < 1$, the rule
$\sqrt\eta\, L_{\varphi, \lambda}$ and the on-site noise cells $\delta_{\cdot, 0}(1 - \eta) 1$
satisfy the channel
condition $\nu + i(\sigma - \sigma(\sqrt\eta L\cdot, \sqrt\eta L\cdot)) \ge 0$; the range of
$(\sqrt\eta L)^{\star T}\Omega(\sqrt\eta L)$ is contained in $\{0\}$; the rule
$\sqrt\eta\, L_{\varphi, \lambda}$ is not symplectic; and for $\lambda \neq 0$ the noise cells
$\nu + (\sqrt\eta L)_*\nu$ of two steps are nonzero at distance $2$. -/
theorem dyn_example_noise (φ lam : ℝ) :
    (∀ g0 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ,
      pullbackKernel (rotationSymbol φ lam) (onSiteKernel g0) 2 =
        (g0 (Sum.inl 0) (Sum.inl 0) * lam ^ 2) •
          Matrix.vecMulVec (Sum.elim (fun _ => Real.sin φ) fun _ => Real.cos φ)
            (Sum.elim (fun _ => Real.sin φ) fun _ => Real.cos φ)) ∧
    (∀ g0 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ, g0.PosSemidef →
      ((∀ x ≠ 0, pullbackKernel (rotationSymbol φ lam) (onSiteKernel g0) x = 0) ↔
        lam = 0 ∨ g0 (Sum.inl 0) (Sum.inl 0) = 0)) ∧
    (lam ≠ 0 → ∀ c : ℝ, 0 < c →
      pullbackKernel (rotationSymbol φ lam) (onSiteKernel (c • 1)) 2 ≠ 0) ∧
    (∀ b : ℝ, 0 ≤ b → ∀ x ≠ 0, pullbackKernel (rotationSymbol φ lam)
      (onSiteKernel (Matrix.diagonal (Sum.elim (fun _ => 0) fun _ => b))) x = 0) ∧
    ∀ η : ℝ, 0 < η → η < 1 →
      IsStateConditionC (cellForm (onSiteKernel ((1 - η) • (1 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1)
          (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ))))
        ((phaseSpace ℤ 1).sigma - ((phaseSpace ℤ 1).sigma).comp
          ((Matrix.toLin' (Real.sqrt η • rotationSymbol φ lam)).restrictScalars ℝ)
          ((Matrix.toLin' (Real.sqrt η • rotationSymbol φ lam)).restrictScalars ℝ)) ∧
      Core.symbolRange (Core.starTranspose (Real.sqrt η • rotationSymbol φ lam) *
        Core.const ℤ (Gaussian.symplecticForm 1) * (Real.sqrt η • rotationSymbol φ lam)) ⊆ {0} ∧
      ¬ IsSymplectic (Real.sqrt η • rotationSymbol φ lam) ∧
      (lam ≠ 0 → (onSiteKernel ((1 - η) • (1 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1)
          (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ)) +
        pullbackKernel (Real.sqrt η • rotationSymbol φ lam)
          (onSiteKernel ((1 - η) • (1 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1)
            (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ)))) 2 ≠ 0) := sorry

open QCALean.Bosonic in
open scoped Matrix ComplexOrder in
/-- The Fourier form of the channel condition (thesis (4.34)) for noise of finite range. Let $M$ be
a symbol on the chain with $n$ modes per site and $(\nu_x)$ cell data of finite range, $\nu_m = 0$
for $|m| > R$. Then the rule $M$ and the noise $\nu$ (the form of the cells) satisfy the channel
condition $\nu + i(\sigma - \sigma(M\cdot, M\cdot)) \ge 0$ exactly when the matrix
$\hat\nu(k) + i\Omega - i\, \hat M(k)^\dagger \Omega \hat M(k)$ is positive semidefinite for every
real $k$, where $\hat M(k)$ is the Fourier symbol of $M$ and ${}^\dagger$ the conjugate
transpose. -/
theorem dyn_fourier_condition {n : ℕ}
    (M : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (AddMonoidAlgebra ℝ ℤ))
    {g : ℤ → Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n) ℝ} (hg : CellData g) (R : ℕ)
    (hR : ∀ m, (R : ℤ) < |m| → g m = 0) :
    IsStateConditionC (cellForm g)
        ((phaseSpace ℤ n).sigma - ((phaseSpace ℤ n).sigma).comp
          ((Matrix.toLin' M).restrictScalars ℝ) ((Matrix.toLin' M).restrictScalars ℝ)) ↔
      ∀ k, (gammaHat g k + Complex.I • (Gaussian.symplecticForm n).map Complex.ofReal -
        Complex.I • ((fourierSymbol k M)ᴴ * (Gaussian.symplecticForm n).map Complex.ofReal *
          fourierSymbol k M)).PosSemidef := sorry

open QCALean.Bosonic in
open scoped Matrix ComplexOrder Pointwise in
/-- Dilations on the chain at the level of covariances (thesis Lemma 4.12, with (4.40)). Let $S$ be
a matrix of Laurent polynomials for $n + m$ modes per site, the $n$ system modes first, with
$S^{\star T}(\Omega_n \oplus \Omega_m) S = \Omega_n \oplus \Omega_m$; let $S_{11}$ be its
system-to-system block and $S_{21}$ its system-to-ancilla block (rows of the ancilla, columns of
the system), and let $\gamma_0$ be a real $2m \times 2m$ matrix with $\gamma_0 + i\Omega_m \ge 0$.
Then the rule $S_{11}$ and the noise of the cells $\nu = S_{21 *}(\delta_{\cdot, 0}\gamma_0)$
satisfy the channel condition $\nu + i(\sigma - \sigma(S_{11}\cdot, S_{11}\cdot)) \ge 0$;
$\hat\nu(k) = \hat S_{21}(k)^\dagger\, \gamma_0\, \hat S_{21}(k)$ for every $k$, with
$\hat S_{21}(k)$ the entrywise evaluation at $z = e^{-ik}$; $N_{S_{11}} \subseteq N_S$; and
$\nu_d = 0$ for $d \notin N_S - N_S$. -/
theorem dyn_dilation {n m : ℕ}
    (S : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n ⊕ Gaussian.PhaseIndex m)
      (Gaussian.PhaseIndex n ⊕ Gaussian.PhaseIndex m) (AddMonoidAlgebra ℝ ℤ))
    (hS : Core.starTranspose S *
        Core.const ℤ (Matrix.fromBlocks (Gaussian.symplecticForm n) 0 0
          (Gaussian.symplecticForm m)) * S =
      Core.const ℤ (Matrix.fromBlocks (Gaussian.symplecticForm n) 0 0
        (Gaussian.symplecticForm m)))
    {γ0 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex m) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex m) ℝ}
    (hγ0 : (γ0.map Complex.ofReal +
      Complex.I • (Gaussian.symplecticForm m).map Complex.ofReal).PosSemidef) :
    IsStateConditionC (cellForm (pullbackKernel S.toBlocks₂₁ (onSiteKernel γ0)))
        ((phaseSpace ℤ n).sigma - ((phaseSpace ℤ n).sigma).comp
          ((Matrix.toLin' S.toBlocks₁₁).restrictScalars ℝ)
          ((Matrix.toLin' S.toBlocks₁₁).restrictScalars ℝ)) ∧
      (∀ k, gammaHat (pullbackKernel S.toBlocks₂₁ (onSiteKernel γ0)) k =
        (S.toBlocks₂₁.map (evalCircle k))ᴴ * γ0.map Complex.ofReal *
          S.toBlocks₂₁.map (evalCircle k)) ∧
      Core.symbolRange S.toBlocks₁₁ ⊆ Core.symbolRange S ∧
      ∀ d ∉ Core.symbolRange S - Core.symbolRange S,
        pullbackKernel S.toBlocks₂₁ (onSiteKernel γ0) d = 0 := sorry

/-! ## Examples

These statements show that some hypotheses of the theorems above can be met (the regime, cell
data with the Fourier condition, noiseless lattice channels) and that the objects are not
degenerate. No Gaussian state is exhibited, and no lattice channel with noise. -/

open scoped Matrix in
/-- The pure-shift case is not empty and is not covered by the other case: the symbol with
$\ell_- = 1$ and $\ell_0 = \ell_+ = 0$ (the shift $z^{-1}$) is symplectic, while $\ell_0 = 0$
does not satisfy $\ell_0^T \Omega \ell_0 = \Omega$. The other case contains symplectic
nearest-neighbour symbols with $\ell_0 \neq 0$ and $\ell_+ = \ell_- \neq 0$. -/
theorem witness_rev_nn_shift :
    (Core.starTranspose (Bosonic.nnSymbol 1 0 0) * Core.const ℤ (Gaussian.symplecticForm 1) *
        Bosonic.nnSymbol 1 0 0 = Core.const ℤ (Gaussian.symplecticForm 1) ∧
      (0 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ)ᵀ *
        Gaussian.symplecticForm 1 * 0 ≠ Gaussian.symplecticForm 1) ∧
    ∃ l0 l : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ, l0 ≠ 0 ∧ l ≠ 0 ∧
      Core.starTranspose (Bosonic.nnSymbol l l0 l) * Core.const ℤ (Gaussian.symplecticForm 1) *
        Bosonic.nnSymbol l l0 l = Core.const ℤ (Gaussian.symplecticForm 1) := sorry

open scoped Matrix ComplexOrder in
/-- The vacuum cells meet the Fourier condition: the one-mode cells $\gamma_0 = 1$ and
$\gamma_x = 0$ for $x \neq 0$ are summable and symmetric, $\hat\gamma(k) = 1$, and
$\hat\gamma(k) + i\Omega$ is positive semidefinite for every real $k$. -/
theorem witness_rev_vacuum_fourier :
    (∀ i j, Summable fun x : ℤ =>
      |(if x = 0 then (1 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ) else 0) i j|) ∧
    (∀ x : ℤ, (if -x = 0 then (1 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ)
      else 0) = (if x = 0 then (1 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ)
        else 0)ᵀ) ∧
    ∀ k : ℝ, Bosonic.gammaHat (fun x : ℤ =>
        if x = 0 then (1 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ) else 0) k =
          1 ∧
      (Bosonic.gammaHat (fun x : ℤ =>
        if x = 0 then (1 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ) else 0) k +
          Complex.I • (Gaussian.symplecticForm 1).map Complex.ofReal).PosSemidef := sorry

/-- An automaton that is not on-site: there is a reversible QCA on the one-mode chain whose rule
does not map the local algebra $\mathcal A(\{0\})$ of the site $0$ into itself. -/
theorem witness_rev_not_onsite :
    ∃ Q : Net.ReversibleQCA (LinearAQFT.Symplectic.ccrNet (Bosonic.phaseSpace ℤ 1))
        (Bosonic.ccrTranslate ℤ 1),
      ¬ (LinearAQFT.Symplectic.ccrNet (Bosonic.phaseSpace ℤ 1) {0}).map Q.rule.toStarAlgHom ≤
        LinearAQFT.Symplectic.ccrNet (Bosonic.phaseSpace ℤ 1) {0} := sorry

open QCALean.Bosonic in
open scoped ComplexOrder in
/-- The hypotheses on parameters and cells can be met: the pair $(\pi/2, 1/2)$ lies in the regime,
with $f_{\rm crit} = 1$ for $\varphi = \pi/2$; and the vacuum cells $\delta_{\cdot, 0}\, 1$ on the
chain with one mode per site are reflection-symmetric cell data with constant Fourier series
$\hat\gamma(k) = 1$, for which $\hat\gamma(k) + i\Omega$ is positive semidefinite for every $k$. -/
theorem witness_dyn_regime_vacuum :
    ExampleRegime (Real.pi / 2) (1 / 2) ∧
    (1 - |Real.cos (Real.pi / 2)|) / |Real.sin (Real.pi / 2)| = 1 ∧
    CellData (onSiteKernel (1 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ)) ∧
    (∀ x, onSiteKernel (1 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ) (-x) =
      onSiteKernel 1 x) ∧
    ∀ k, gammaHat (onSiteKernel (1 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℝ))
        k = 1 ∧
      ((1 : Matrix (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1) ℂ) +
        Complex.I • (Gaussian.symplecticForm 1).map Complex.ofReal).PosSemidef := sorry

open QCALean.Bosonic in
/-- Lattice channels exist for the example rule: for all real $\varphi$, $\lambda$ there is a
Gaussian channel $T$ on the CCR algebra of the chain with one mode per site that is a lattice
channel with rule $L_{\varphi, \lambda}$ and noise cells $0$, with
$T(W(u)) = W(L_{\varphi, \lambda} u)$ for every $u$. -/
theorem witness_dyn_lattice_channel (φ lam : ℝ) :
    ∃ T : GaussianChannel (phaseSpace ℤ 1).sigma, IsLatticeChannel T (rotationSymbol φ lam) 0 ∧
      ∀ u : Core.LatticeSpace ℝ ℤ (Gaussian.PhaseIndex 1),
        T.map (wCCR (phaseSpace ℤ 1).sigma u) =
          wCCR (phaseSpace ℤ 1).sigma (Matrix.toLin' (rotationSymbol φ lam) u) := sorry

